09:48:05 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['European Union law\n1. European Union law includes Treaties and Regulations, which have direct effect when they are clear, unconditional, and immediate.', 'European Union law\n2. Directives, on the other hand, typically do not give citizens standing to sue other citizens for non-compliance.', 'European Union law\n3. This is because Directives are directed towards member states, and they generally allow member states the freedom to choose the form and methods for implementing them.', 'European Union law\n4. However, member states may impose higher standards than what is required by Directives. For example, the Working Time Directive requires workers to have at least 4 weeks paid holiday, but most member states provide for more than 28 days in national law.', 'European Union law\n5. Citizens can make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but they cannot directly sue others based on the Directives themselves.', 'European Union law\n6. Directives do not have "horizontal" direct effect, meaning they cannot create rights and duties between non-state parties.', 'European Union law\n7. The view that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens was instantly controversial, but the Court of Justice refused this interpretation.', 'European Union law\n8. There are five large exceptions to the general principle that citizens cannot directly sue others based on Directives.', 'European Union law\n9. Directives often establish minimum standards, allowing member states to impose higher standards based on national laws.', 'European Union law\n10. The lack of direct effect of Directives can lead to inequalities between member states and citizens in terms of access to legal remedies.', 'European Union law\nWhile the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nWhat generally does not allow citizens to sue other citizens?', 'answer': 'Directives'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immediate), Directives do not generally give citizens (as opposed to the member state) standing to sue other citizens. In theory, this is because TFEU article 288 says Directives are addressed to the member states and usually "leave to the national authorities the choice of form and methods" to implement. In part this reflects that directives often create minimum standards, leaving member states to apply higher standards. For example, the Working Time Directive requires that every worker has at least 4 weeks paid holidays each year, but most member states require more than 28 days in national law. However, on the current position adopted by the Court of Justice, citizens have standing to make claims based on national laws that implement Directives, but not from Directives themselves. Directives do not have so called "horizontal" direct effect (i.e. between non-state parties). This view was instantly controversial, and in the early 1990s three Advocate Generals persuasively argued that Directives should create rights and duties for all citizens. The Court of Justice refused, but there are five large exceptions.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nHow many paid holiday days does the Working Time directive require workers to have each year?', 'answer': '4 weeks'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'While the Treaties and Regulations will have direct effect (if clear, unconditional and immedi