10:05:13 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Doctor Who\n1. Regeneration allows the Doctor Who franchise to adapt to changes in the actor's physical health.", "Doctor Who\n2. Recasting of the main character has been a recurring theme in the show's history.", 'Doctor Who\n3. The idea of regeneration was not immediately introduced, but instead evolved over time.', 'Doctor Who\n4. The term "regeneration" was not coined until the third on-screen regeneration of the Doctor.', 'Doctor Who\n5. The term "renewal" was initially used to describe the first regeneration of the Doctor, and "change of appearance" for the second.', "Doctor Who\n6. The concept of regeneration enables the depiction of alternative Doctors from the character's relative past or future.", 'Doctor Who\n7. Regeneration is a device used to permit the recasting of the main character.', 'Doctor Who\nProducers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term is used to explain a change in the appearance of Doctor Who?', 'answer': 'regeneration'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhen was the term "regeneration" first used?', 'answer': "the Doctor's third on-screen regeneration"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat first prompted the regeneration concept?', 'answer': "William Hartnell's poor health"}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term was used for the first regeneration?', 'answer': 'renewal'}, {'title': 'Doctor Who', 'context': 'Producers introduced the concept of regeneration to permit the recasting of the main character. This was first prompted by original star William Hartnell\'s poor health. The actual term "regeneration" was not initially conceived of until the Doctor\'s third on-screen regeneration however; Hartnell\'s Doctor had merely described undergoing a "renewal," and the Second Doctor underwent a "change of appearance".[citation needed] The device has allowed for the recasting of the actor various times in the show\'s history, as well as the depiction of alternative Doctors either from the Doctor\'s relative past or future.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Doctor Who\nWhat term was used for the second regeneration?', 'answer': 'change of appearance'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
