10:10:00 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Chloroplast\n1. Chloroplasts play a crucial role in photosynthesis, specifically through photosystems II and I.', 'Chloroplast\n2. Photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize electrons.', 'Chloroplast\n3. Photosystem I reenergizes electrons at the end of an electron transport chain, normally accepting NADP+ but sometimes also contributing to cyclic photophosphorylation.', 'Chloroplast\n4. Cyclic photophosphorylation is an alternative energy generation pathway in chloroplasts.', 'Chloroplast\n5. Cyclic photophosphorylation is more common in C4 plants, which require higher ATP production than NADPH.', 'Chloroplast\n6. Chloroplasts maintain the thylakoid space at a high H+ concentration, which is necessary for the generation of ATP through electron transport chains.', 'Chloroplast\nWhile photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat usually takes reenergized electrons?', 'answer': 'NADP+'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nIn what process are electrons recycled?', 'answer': 'cyclic photophosphorylation'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhere is cyclic photophosphorylation common?', 'answer': 'in C4 plants'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do C4 plants need?', 'answer': 'more ATP than NADPH'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
