10:11:52 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Normans\n1. Normans had a long-standing relationship with the English due to their shared history, cultural exchanges, and military encounters.', 'Normans\n2. The Normans and the English were in contact long before any formal alliances were formed.', 'Normans\n3. Close ties of blood were formed between the two kingdoms as a result of the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, to King Ethelred II of England.', 'Normans\n4. Duke Richard II of Normandy had a deep connection with King Ethelred II of England, as they shared a blood relation through his sister.', 'Normans\n5. Ethelred II of England, also known as the unready, eventually fled to Normandy, where he stayed until 1016, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard.', "Normans\n6. Ethelred's time spent in Normandy significantly influenced his perspective and those of his children.", "Normans\n7. The treaty of Stow, signed in 1013, resulted in Cnut the Great's conquest of England, which effectively ended Ethelred's reign.", 'Normans\n8. Normans played a significant role in the politics of both England and France, due to their varied experiences and personal relationships with English and French leaders.', 'Normans\n9. The Norman influence on English culture and language was long-lasting and profound, as seen in the migration of Norman aristocrats into England.', 'Normans\n10. In the end, the Normans and English were connected by blood, shared history, and political alliances, which contributed to the development of English culture and society.', 'Normans\n74. Recently, American studies of the Middle Ages have begun to take "economic explanations" a bit more seriously. The "high medieval mind" is currently being rewritten to emphasize the strengths and successes of medieval economic life, instead of its weaknesses and failures. In view of the fact that the English vocabulary for monetary matters had its origin in a Scandinavian language area, the stronger international links to other northern European countries, especially after 1066, contribute to an over-simplified depiction of the economy, which is apparent in the syllabus\' generic character and its generalizations. Important regional differences exist, which can only be appreciated with the aid of the kind of database now being created for this study.', 'Normans\nThe passage suggests that there are several implications derived from the content, such as:', 'Normans\n1. Recent studies on American Middle Ages have started to consider economic explanations more seriously, moving away from the traditional view of the weaknesses and failures of medieval economic life.', 'Normans\n2. The medieval period is being reassessed in a more favorable light as the "high medieval mind," indicating a shift in scholarly perspectives.', 'Normans\n3. The influence of economic factors and the growth of trade networks in medieval England are now more comprehensively acknowledged.', 'Normans\n4. The connections to the Scandinavian language area and the development of the English vocabulary for monetary matters demonstrate how cultural influences shape economic terms.', 'Normans\n5. The stronger international links to other northern European countries, particularly after the Norman Conquest in 1066, contribute to an oversimplified depiction of medieval economic life.', "Normans\n6. The syllabus' generic character and its generalizations tend to ignore the important regional differences that existed in medieval England.", 'Normans\n7. The creation of databases for such studies can help to better appreciate the regional differences and nuances in economic life that have been overlooked in the past.', 'Normans\n8. It is essential for researchers to employ a more nuanced approach to understanding the Middle Ages, accounting for local variations and the unique historical context of each region.', 'Normans\n9. The shift in perspectives on medieval economic life is indicative of broader changes in the scholarly field that seek to provide more comprehensive and accurate historical accounts.', 'Normans\nThe passage should be read as follows:', 'Normans\nA brief history of comments.', "Normans\nOther parts of the history of the use of the Software Platform model, but not the other Software objects, are in this article's talk page.", 'Normans\nThe model did not yet have an official name. But on this conversation, on Sep. 1997, users designated the platform-like object on use files as a "Software Platform" (the more grammatical "software platform", "software system", "computer system", or "computer platform" were not used, but rather "software-platform"). In early 1998, Mike Hebert put a link on the "Models" page to "A software Platform" in this forum (as a main link) and in "subarticles" under the Software Platform.', 'Normans\nThe notes below to it are from Dec 1997 to Sep 1998. Usefull of discussion about the relationship of the Software Platform to other objects, on the inclusion of SoftwarePlatform instances (for reuse) in other object definitions, etc.', 'Normans\nIn this material, different meanings are given to the "build" activity, in relation to the requirements development and the decision about using existing, available, software (available sources, or the realization of new software, both in the sense of specifications or the code by the Software Platform itself). And the difference about the viewpoint of "Software Components on a System" that are either SystemSoftware or SW on own (with obvious functionality), or are provided by others (which may or may not have their own reasons to provide their own SW.', "Normans\n1. Description: software is the record of a specific set of solutions or decisions to the problems found in the system. These solutions can be in the form of written code, made up of the software's instructions, data, and documentation. This description highlights the importance of understanding software as not just the code itself, but also as the process of problem-solving and decision-making involved in creating and using it.", 'Normans\n2. Understanding of Software: Software can be viewed as a collection of instructions or code that directs the operation of hardware. It encompasses a wide range of tools, applications, and tools used to develop other applications. This emphasizes the versatility and critical role of software in enabling the functionality of hardware and facilitating various tasks.', 'Normans\n3. Use of software components: Software components are fundamental units that combine functions to develop complex software systems. They enable engineers to maintain existing software while integrating new software components in modular fashion. This modular approach supports easier updates, upgrades, and troubleshooting, ultimately contributing to the overall efficiency and maintainability of software systems.', 'Normans\n4. High-level description: This model is a high-level description of available software in a certain context, where the complex software is built. It differs from explicit functional models by focusing on providing an overview of what is available rather than detailing specific functions or features. This allows for easier decision-making regarding which software components to incorporate into a software platform.', 'Normans\n5. Software Platform and SW components: The software platform, or software system, encompasses the components required to build complex software. These components are combined to form a comprehensive platform that supports the development and execution of various software applications. The inclusion of predefined SW components in these platforms provides a convenient and efficient way to integrate essential functions and tools into software development projects.', 'Normans\n6. Designing software systems: Understanding the nature of software and the role of software components is crucial when designing software systems. It allows for the identification of the most appropriate components to use, ensuring that the software system is reliable, efficient, and tailored to meet specific requirements. This knowledge enables developers to make informed decisions and create software systems that align with their goals and objectives.', 'Normans\n7. Hierarchical structure: The Software Platform model and its associated software components form a hierarchical structure. The software platform serves as the foundation for other software components, which in turn can be built upon to create more complex software systems. This hierarchical organization facilitates easy expansion, reusability, and maintenance of software systems, as components can be easily integrated or removed without disrupting the entire system.', 'Normans\n8. Benefits of using a Software Platform: By leveraging a Software Platform and its pre-defined software components, developers can streamline the software development process. This approach reduces the need for extensive custom code and simplifies the integration of various software elements. The result is a more efficient, maintainable, and scalable software system that can be quickly deployed and adapted to meet evolving requirements.', 'Normans\nIn summary, the Software Platform model and the understanding of software and its components play a vital role in the development of complex software systems. By recognizing software as a set of solutions and decision-making processes, using modular and reusable software components, and adopting a hierarchical approach, developers can create software systems that are efficient, reliable, and tailored to specific needs. The Software Platform model serves as a valuable foundation for integrating existing software components, allowing for easy expansion, maintenance, and adaptation to changing requirements.', 'Normans\nDoes the quote has any faulty conclusions?', 'Normans\n" However, the Caste proscription is not yet final and the Republican party will be moving to reinstate it, citing its provisions as salutary, necessary and for the betterment of society."', 'Normans\n" Not only are there threats to the security of the nation and society, but we are reminded that the Cabinet made many bad decisions in the past as a result of Islamic terrorism. Today, many new ideas are being introduced under the guise of Islamic-terrrorism paranoia. These include new pre-election certification procedures and military laws of some very undemocratic nature."', 'Normans\n"A chill of fear runs through the political body as the Republican party begins to tell its followers: If you vote Democrat, you will be traumatized, even raped by wild gangs of African-Americans and Hispanic Americans in the night, or perhaps you will be forced to pay for a pervert’s night time snuff-film session, or some will rape your children."', 'Normans\n"It is alleged that repressive measures have been recently passed for the sole purpose of crushing the opposition at any expense."', 'Normans\nThe passage appears to have several faulty conclusions. Firstly, the statement that the Caste proscription is not yet final and will be reinstated seems to be based on speculation or hearsay rather than actual evidence. Additionally, citing its provisions as salutary and necessary for the betterment of society may be unfounded and subjective.', 'Normans\nThe claim that there are threats to the security of the nation and society, especially in the context of Islamic terrorism, lacks sufficient context and evidence to support this assertion. Similarly, the introduction of new ideas and', "Normans\nThe Normans were in contact with England from an early date. Not only were their original Viking brethren still ravaging the English coasts, they occupied most of the important ports opposite England across the English Channel. This relationship eventually produced closer ties of blood through the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, and King Ethelred II of England. Because of this, Ethelred fled to Normandy in 1013, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard. His stay in Normandy (until 1016) influenced him and his sons by Emma, who stayed in Normandy after Cnut the Great's conquest of the isle."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Normans', 'context': "The Normans were in contact with England from an early date. Not only were their original Viking brethren still ravaging the English coasts, they occupied most of the important ports opposite England across the English Channel. This relationship eventually produced closer ties of blood through the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, and King Ethelred II of England. Because of this, Ethelred fled to Normandy in 1013, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard. His stay in Normandy (until 1016) influenced him and his sons by Emma, who stayed in Normandy after Cnut the Great's conquest of the isle.", 'question': 'Topic: Normans\nWho did Emma Marry?', 'answer': 'King Ethelred II'}, {'title': 'Normans', 'context': "The Normans were in contact with England from an early date. Not only were their original Viking brethren still ravaging the English coasts, they occupied most of the important ports opposite England across the English Channel. This relationship eventually produced closer ties of blood through the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, and King Ethelred II of England. Because of this, Ethelred fled to Normandy in 1013, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard. His stay in Normandy (until 1016) influenced him and his sons by Emma, who stayed in Normandy after Cnut the Great's conquest of the isle.", 'question': "Topic: Normans\nWho was Emma's brother?", 'answer': 'Duke Richard II'}, {'title': 'Normans', 'context': "The Normans were in contact with England from an early date. Not only were their original Viking brethren still ravaging the English coasts, they occupied most of the important ports opposite England across the English Channel. This relationship eventually produced closer ties of blood through the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, and King Ethelred II of England. Because of this, Ethelred fled to Normandy in 1013, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard. His stay in Normandy (until 1016) influenced him and his sons by Emma, who stayed in Normandy after Cnut the Great's conquest of the isle.", 'question': 'Topic: Normans\nTo where did Ethelred flee?', 'answer': 'Normandy'}, {'title': 'Normans', 'context': "The Normans were in contact with England from an early date. Not only were their original Viking brethren still ravaging the English coasts, they occupied most of the important ports opposite England across the English Channel. This relationship eventually produced closer ties of blood through the marriage of Emma, sister of Duke Richard II of Normandy, and King Ethelred II of England. Because of this, Ethelred fled to Normandy in 1013, when he was forced from his kingdom by Sweyn Forkbeard. His stay in Normandy (until 1016) influenced him and his sons by Emma, who stayed in Normandy after Cnut the Great's conquest of the isle.", 'question': 'Topic: Normans\nWho kicked Ethelred out?', 'answer': 'Sweyn Forkbeard'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
