10:19:46 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Fresno, California\n1. The Alta Vista Tract underwent rapid development after the Fresno Traction Company established a right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, providing transportation links to downtown and the County Hospital.', 'Fresno, California\n2. The subdivision became a part of the City of Fresno in 1912, through a public election where women were allowed to vote for the first time in the area.', 'Fresno, California\n3. Alta Vista Tract, officially mapped in 1911, expanded on 190 acres of land that had been previously used for an alfalfa field.', 'Fresno, California\n4. The subdivision was prepared for real estate development by the Pacific Improvement Corporation, following which William Stranahan and the corporation officially recorded and platted the land in 1911.', 'Fresno, California\n5. The construction of 267 homes in the subdivision within a period of only five years indicates the demand for residential housing in the area.', 'Fresno, California\n6. Before development, the land was solely used for agricultural purposes, specifically for growing alfalfa. This points out that there might have been a transformation from agriculture to residential and urban purposes.', 'Fresno, California\n7. The area was a heavily developed section of the city, reflected by the number of large, stately homes that lined its boulevard.', "Fresno, California\n8. The suburb's water supply was ensured by municipal improvements, such as the provision of water services.", 'Fresno, California\n9. The presence of the Fresno Traction Company led to the construction and early development of the area, likely providing a boost to real estate prices and attracting buyers.', 'Fresno, California\n10. A number of prominent historical figures might have lived in the large, stately homes in the Alta Vista Tract, given its attractiveness and locations to essential facilities.', "Fresno, California\nHomes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in which women voted in the community. At the time of its admission to the City, the Alta Vista Tract was uninhabited but landscaped, although the trees had to be watered by tank wagon. In 1914 developers Billings & Meyering acquired the tract, completed street development, provided the last of the necessary municipal improvements including water service, and began marketing the property with fervor. A mere half decade later the tract had 267 homes. This rapid development was no doubt hastened by the Fresno Traction Company right-of-way along Huntington Boulevard, which provided streetcar connections between downtown and the County Hospital.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nWhich boulevard can you find many majestic homes in the area?', 'answer': 'Huntington Boulevard'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "Homes from the early 20th century line this boulevard in the heart of the historic Alta Vista Tract. The section of Huntington Boulevard between First Street on the west to Cedar Avenue on the east is the home to many large, stately homes. The original development of this area began circa 1910, on 190 acres of what had been an alfalfa field. The Alta Vista Tract, as the land would become known, was mapped by William Stranahan for the Pacific Improvement Corporation, and was officially platted in 1911. The tract's boundaries were Balch Avenue on the south, Cedar Avenue on the east, the rear property line of Platt Avenue (east of Sixth Street) and Platt Avenue (west of Sixth Street) on the north, and First Street on the west. The subdivision was annexed to the City in January 1912, in an election that was the first in