10:51:36 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Steam engine\n1. Steam engines rely on multiple mechanisms to ensure safe operation.', 'Steam engine\n2. One independent mechanism may be manually adjusted by the user.', 'Steam engine\n3. Another mechanism is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe.', 'Steam engine\n4. Safety valves historically used a lever-operated valve restrained by a weight or spring.', 'Steam engine\n5. Driver-adjustable safety valves could be dangerous due to tampering.', 'Steam engine\n6. More recent safety valves use spring-loaded valves with adjustable springs and security seals.', 'Steam engine\n7. Tampering with the adjustment of safety valves can lead to accidents.', 'Steam engine\n8. Safety valves are crucial for preventing boiler explosions and maintaining the safety of steam engines.', 'Steam engine\nSteam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'Steam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nHow many mechanisms does a typical steam engine have to keep boiler pressure from getting too high?', 'answer': 'two'}, {'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'Steam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nWhat is restrained with a lever in the top of a boiler?', 'answer': 'plug valve'}, {'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'Steam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nWhat type of valve is used by recent safety valves?', 'answer': 'adjustable spring-loaded'}, {'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'Steam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nIn an adjustable spring-loaded valve, what needs to be broken to allow an operator to tamper with it?', 'answer': 'seal'}, {'title': 'Steam engine', 'context': 'Steam engines frequently possess two independent mechanisms for ensuring that the pressure in the boiler does not go too high; one may be adjusted by the user, the second is typically designed as an ultimate fail-safe. Such safety valves traditionally used a simple lever to restrain a plug valve in the top of a boiler. One end of the lever carried a weight or spring that restrained the valve against steam pressure. Early valves could be adjusted by engine drivers, leading to many accidents when a driver fastened the valve down to allow greater steam pressure and more power from the engine. The more recent type of safety valve uses an adjustable spring-loaded valve, which is locked such that operators may not tamper with its adjustment unless a seal illegally is broken. This arrangement is considerably safer.[citation needed]', 'question': 'Topic: Steam engine\nAlong with a desire for more steam pressure, what were early drivers looking to generate when they fastened safety valves down?', 'answer': 'more power'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
