10:58:09 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['French and Indian War\n1. The French and Indian War involved multiple ethnic groups and tribes from various parts of North America, with some supporting the French, while others supported the British.', 'French and Indian War\n2. The French utilized their trading relationships with various tribes in order to enlist support and fighters for the war.', 'French and Indian War\n3. The British sought support from the Iroquois Six Nations and the Cherokee, with the latter eventually joining the war on the side of the French and their trading partners.', 'French and Indian War\n4. The Treaty of Easton in 1758 demonstrates the diplomatic efforts made by the Pennsylvania government to secure neutrality in the conflict from northern tribes in exchange for land concessions and other benefits.', 'French and Indian War\n5. Conflicts among tribes were not uncommon, as small bands could participate in warfare on the "other side" of the conflict from their formally negotiated agreements.', 'French and Indian War\n6. The involvement of multiple ethnic groups and tribes in the war indicates the significance of inter-tribal relations and alliances during this period in North American history.', 'French and Indian War\n7. The French and British were both involved in diplomatic efforts to secure the support of the Creek and Cherokee, highlighting their importance in the conflict.', 'French and Indian War\n8. The French were considered the primary trading partners and suppliers of arms for many of the tribes involved in the war, highlighting the significance of economic relations in the conflict.', 'French and Indian War\n9. The outcomes of the war were influenced by the alliances and relationships formed between tribes and European powers, further highlighting the impact of inter-tribal relations and European influence on the conflict.', 'French and Indian War\n10. The conflict demonstrated the complex and interconnected nature of the relationships between European powers and Native American tribes during this period of North American history.', 'French and Indian War\nFurther south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes region (an area not directly subject to the conflict between the French and British), including the Huron, Mississauga, Ojibwa, Winnebago, and Potawatomi. The British were supported in the war by the Iroquois Six Nations, and also by the Cherokee – until differences sparked the Anglo-Cherokee War in 1758. In 1758 the Pennsylvania government successfully negotiated the Treaty of Easton, in which a number of tribes in the Ohio Country promised neutrality in exchange for land concessions and other considerations. Most of the other northern tribes sided with the French, their primary trading partner and supplier of arms. The Creek and Cherokee were subject to diplomatic efforts by both the French and British to gain either their support or neutrality in the conflict. It was not uncommon for small bands to participate on the "other side" of the conflict from formally negotiated agreements, as most tribes were decentralized and bands made their own decisions about warfare.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'French and Indian War', 'context': 'Further south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes region (an area not directly subject to the conflict between the French and British), including the Huron, Mississauga, Ojibwa, Winnebago, and Potawatomi. The British were supported in the war by the Iroquois Six Nations, and also by the Cherokee – until differences sparked the Anglo-Cherokee War in 1758. In 1758 the Pennsylvania government successfully negotiated the Treaty of Easton, in which a number of tribes in the Ohio Country promised neutrality in exchange for land concessions and other considerations. Most of the other northern tribes sided with the French, their primary trading partner and supplier of arms. The Creek and Cherokee were subject to diplomatic efforts by both the French and British to gain either their support or neutrality in the conflict. It was not uncommon for small bands to participate on the "other side" of the conflict from formally negotiated agreements, as most tribes were decentralized and bands made their own decisions about warfare.', 'question': 'Topic: French and Indian War\nWhat are the Siouan-speaking tribes?', 'answer': 'Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw'}, {'title': 'French and Indian War', 'context': 'Further south the Southeast interior was dominated by Siouan-speaking Catawba, Muskogee-speaking Creek and Choctaw, and the Iroquoian-speaking Cherokee tribes. When war broke out, the French used their trading connections to recruit fighters from tribes in western portions of the Great Lakes region (an area not directly subject to the conflict between the French and British), including the Huron, Mississauga, Ojibwa, Winnebago, and Potawatomi. The British were supported in the war by the Iroquois Six Nations, and also by the Cherokee – until differences sparked the Anglo-Cherokee War in 1758. In 1758 the Pennsylvania government successfully negotiated the Treaty of Easton, in which a number of tribes in the Ohio Country promised neutrality in exchange for land concessions and other considerations. Most of the other northern tribes sided with the French, their primary trading partner and supplier of arms. The Creek and Cherokee were subject to diplomatic efforts by both the French and British to gain either their support or neut