11:07:41 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['University of Chicago\n1. The University of Chicago is a prestigious institution that has contributed significantly to various fields of study and research.', 'University of Chicago\n2. The university is known for its innovative and groundbreaking experiments in economics, sociology, physics, chemistry, and neurophysiology.', "University of Chicago\n3. The free market economic theories supported by the University of Chicago's Chicago School of Economics have had a significant impact on the global economy.", 'University of Chicago\n4. The university has also been a major contributor to the development of sociology, with its sociological research shaping the discipline in the United States.', 'University of Chicago\n5. The University of Chicago has been a leading institution in the field of physics, with notable experiments and discoveries, including the Chicago Pile-1 nuclear reactor and the oil-drop experiment.', 'University of Chicago\n6. The radiocarbon dating method developed by the university has revolutionized archaeology and anthropology, providing crucial insights into human history and evolution.', 'University of Chicago\n7. The Miller-Urey experiment conducted at the university has shed light on the origins of life and provided important evidence for the theory of abiogenesis.', 'University of Chicago\n8. The discovery of REM sleep at the university in 1953 has contributed to our understanding of sleep patterns and has implications for the treatment of sleep disorders.', "University of Chicago\nThe University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat role in economics did the university play a major part in?', 'answer': 'shaping ideas about the free market'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat was the name of the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction?', 'answer': 'Chicago Pile-1'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhat was the name of the experiment that tested how life originated?', 'answer': 'Miller–Urey experiment'}, {'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.", 'question': 'Topic: University of Chicago\nWhen was REM sleep discovered?', 'answer': '1953'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
