ted temperature, and monitored the crew's vital signs.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a propulsion system that allowed it to change course and control the spacecraft's attitude.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a navigation and guidance system to allow the crew to navigate and land on the lunar surface.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a communications system that allowed the crew to communicate with mission control on Earth.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a life support system that provided oxygen, regulated temperature, and monitored the crew's vital signs.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a propulsion system that allowed it to change course and control the spacecraft's attitude.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a navigation and guidance system to allow the crew to navigate and land on the lunar surface.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a communications system that allowed the crew to communicate with mission control on Earth.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a life support system that provided oxygen, regulated temperature, and monitored the crew's vital signs.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a propulsion system that allowed it to change course and control the spacecraft's attitude.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a navigation and guidance system to allow the crew to navigate and land on the lunar surface.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a communications system that allowed the crew to communicate with mission control on Earth.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a life support system that provided oxygen, regulated temperature, and monitored the crew's vital signs.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a propulsion system that allowed it to change course and control the spacecraft's attitude.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a navigation and guidance system to allow the crew to navigate and land on the lunar surface.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a communications system that allowed the crew to communicate with mission control on Earth.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a life support system that provided oxygen, regulated temperature, and monitored the crew's vital signs.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a propulsion system that allowed it to change course and control the spacecraft's attitude.", "Apollo program\n- The mission's command service module included a navigation and guidance system to allow the crew to navigate and land on the lunar surface.", 'Apollo program\n-', 'Apollo program\nIn November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nCharles Conrad and Alan Bean were on what spacecraft to the moon?', 'answer': 'Apollo 12'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': "Topic: Apollo program\nWhich lunar probe was near the Apollo 12 crew's landing site?", 'answer': 'Surveyor 3'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nWhat did the crew of Apollo 12 do with parts of the Surveyor they landed near after photographing them?', 'answer': 'returned to Earth'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'In November 1969, Gemini veteran Charles "Pete" Conrad and rookie Alan L. Bean made a precision landing on Apollo 12 within walking distance of the Surveyor 3 unmanned lunar probe, which had landed in April 1967 on the Ocean of Storms. The Command Module Pilot was Gemini veteran Richard F. Gordon, Jr. Conrad and Bean carried the first lunar surface color television camera, but it was damaged when accidentally pointed into the Sun. They made two EVAs totaling 7 hours and 45 minutes. On one, they walked to the Surveyor, photographed it, and removed some parts which they returned to Earth.', 'question': 'Topic: Apollo program\nWhat damaged the color TV camera that Apollo 12 had taken into space?', 'answer': 'the Sun'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
