11:16:41 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Rhine\n1. Human impact on the Rhine delta can be traced back to ~3000 years before present (BP).', 'Rhine\n2. Increased land clearance in central Germany during the Bronze Age led to a higher sediment load in the Rhine.', 'Rhine\n3. The heightened sediment load resulted in a faster rate of delta growth and an increased frequency of flooding.', 'Rhine\n4. This accelerated sedimentation reduced peat formation in the delta.', 'Rhine\n5. The main process of distributing sediment across the subrecent delta over the past 6000 years was avulsion, where river channels changed course to new locations on the floodplain.', 'Rhine\n6. There have been approximately 80 avulsions in the Rhine delta over the past 6000 years.', 'Rhine\n7. Direct human impact began in the delta with peat mining for salt and fuel, starting from Roman times.', 'Rhine\n8. Embankment of major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries took place in the 11-13th century AD.', 'Rhine\n9. Canals, bend shortening, and groynes were built to prevent river channel migration and sediment buildup.', "Rhine\n10. The Rhine delta's history is a result of both natural processes and human interventions, highlighting the importance of understanding the interplay between human activity and environmental change.", "Rhine\nSince ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Rhine', 'context': "Since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up.", 'question': 'Topic: Rhine\nWhen have humans started impacting the delta?', 'answer': '3000 yr BP'}, {'title': 'Rhine', 'context': "Since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up.", 'question': 'Topic: Rhine\nWhat has increased sediment and delta growth also produced in the Rhine?', 'answer': 'increased flooding and sedimentation'}, {'title': 'Rhine', 'context': "Since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up.", 'question': 'Topic: Rhine\nWhat increased in the Rhine because of the land clearance in the upland areas?', 'answer': 'sediment load'}, {'title': 'Rhine', 'context': "Since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up.", 'question': 'Topic: Rhine\nWhen did embankment of the major Rhine distributaries take palce?', 'answer': '11–13th century'}, {'title': 'Rhine', 'context': "Since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta. Over the past 6000 years, approximately 80 avulsions have occurred. Direct human impact in the delta started with peat mining, for salt and fuel, from Roman times onward. This was followed by embankment, of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries, which took place in the 11–13th century AD. Thereafter, canals were dug, bends were short cut and groynes were built, to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up.", 'question': 'Topic: Rhine\nHow many avulsions have occurred in the past 6000 yeas?', 'answer': '80'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs